                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the closed process on two dimensions`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Strategy at Chimiq has been made by <strong>six people behind a closed door</strong> for thirty years; the rest of the firm receives cascaded numbers it did not help build and does not believe. Growth has been flat at 1–2% against a 4% market. Using the open-strategy lens, separate the two dimensions of openness — <strong>inclusion</strong> (who participates in making strategy) and <strong>transparency</strong> (who sees what information) — and decide which Chimiq most lacks. Name the real cost of closure and what closure genuinely protects. The trap: assuming you must open <em>everything to everyone</em>, or that slicker communication of the same six-person plan will create ownership.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Decide how much to disclose, and to whom`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the participation-and-disclosure architecture for the next cycle. Choose how broadly to <strong>include</strong> — a structured hub-director forum, a gated strategy jam, or open it to all 1,300 — then build the <strong>disclosure matrix</strong>: for each data class, decide Open, Gated or Closed and to whom. The PE confidentiality directive and the covenant at 2.9x are binding: <em>margin-by-customer and the M&A pipeline move the bank and the competitor</em>, while hub-level cost and growth data can build ownership if shared with managers. Fund real facilitation — participation without it is theatre.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Design the performance-measurement dashboard`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Build the management-control instrument that runs the opened strategy. Choose the <strong>architecture</strong> — a Balanced-Scorecard cascade, an OKR commitment system or a hybrid — and the <strong>Simons levers</strong> it emphasises. Decide how many of the 6–8 measures are <strong>shared widely</strong> to build ownership and how many stay <strong>board-only</strong> to preserve control, and embed a <strong>calibration rule</strong> against the 11% forecast bias. The trap: a 40-metric, board-only diagnostic that scores the hubs but never lets them <em>steer</em> — reproducing the disengagement you set out to fix.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Present to the board and recover`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present calibrated openness to the PE board chair (who fears leaks before refinancing) and the founder-CEO (who fears losing control). Then the recovery test lands: <em>the opened plan leaks to a trade journal</em>, and the bottom-up targets come in <em>4% below the refinancing requirement</em>. Treat the leak as the <strong>architecture problem</strong> it is — find the gate that failed and re-gate it without re-closing the participation that finally created ownership — and close the 4% <strong>with</strong> the hub directors, not over them. Defend the calibration against both extremes.`,

