                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_inclusion:     `<strong>That is the sharper read.</strong> Chimiq does not lack information so much as <em>participation</em> — the people closest to customers and costs never help build the plan, so it misses reality and nobody owns it. Inclusion is the dimension you most lack; transparency is a calibrated second move under the directive.`,
                    coach_r1_transparency:  `<strong>More visibility alone won't fix this.</strong> People can already see the cascaded numbers — they just had no hand in making them, so they don't believe them. The deficit is inclusion, not transparency, and over-disclosing under a confidentiality directive adds leak risk for little ownership gain.`,
                    coach_r1_bothmax:       `<strong>Maximal on both dimensions is the classic trap.</strong> Open everything to everyone and you energise the firm right up to the moment margin-by-customer and the M&A pipeline reach a competitor and the bank mid-refinancing. Open strategy is calibrated, not maximal — which classes did you deliberately keep closed?`,
                    coach_r1_neither:       `<strong>A slicker cascade of the same six-person plan is not a fix.</strong> Your pulse says 19% believe the targets are achievable and forecasts run 11% hot. Better communication of a plan nobody helped build does not create ownership. What did you change about <em>who makes</em> the strategy?`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named inclusion as the missing dimension AND located the real cost of closure in ownership and forecast quality — while still protecting the confidentiality the PE directive requires. That is the calibrated lens the whole redesign depends on.`,
                    coach_r1_costwrong:     `<strong>That misreads what closure costs you.</strong> The closed process is decisive and confidential — those are its strengths. What it destroys is ownership and forecast quality: targets nobody helped set, run 11% hot. Name the cost that the pulse and the forecast bias actually show.`,
                    coach_r1_protectnothing:`<strong>Closure does protect something real.</strong> Under the PE directive, margin-by-customer and the M&A pipeline are genuinely board-sensitive — open them and you raise leak and covenant risk. "Open every data class" is exactly how the board revokes your mandate.`,
                    coach_r1_hierarchy:     `<strong>Protecting executive privilege is protecting the disease.</strong> Strategy as a leadership privilege is precisely what produced 24%/19% on the pulse. The thing worth protecting is confidentiality of board-sensitive data — not the closed door itself.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandour:    `<strong>This is how the redesign becomes window-dressing.</strong> Low willingness to name the participation failure means you'll relabel a cascade as "open strategy" and change nothing about who makes the plan. Name it honestly or you repeat the flat year.`,
                    coach_r2_forum:         `<strong>A structured hub-director forum is well-calibrated inclusion.</strong> It brings the people closest to costs and customers into making the plan — high ownership — without the noise and leak exposure of opening everything to 1,300. This is the inclusion lever used deliberately.`,
                    coach_r2_jamgated:      `<strong>A gated strategy jam can work.</strong> Inviting hundreds to contribute on bounded topics harvests real signal and ownership while keeping board-sensitive classes off the table — provided your disclosure matrix actually holds.`,
                    coach_r2_jamopen:       `<strong>Opening everything to all 1,300 is radical transparency.</strong> Energising — until the margin and pipeline data on the open jam reach a competitor and the bank asks why your sensitive data is on a trade forum mid-refinancing. Inclusion need not mean total disclosure; calibrate it.`,
                    coach_r2_closed:        `<strong>Cascading the same plan more clearly changes nothing.</strong> If the six people still make the strategy, you reproduce the ownership deficit you diagnosed. Inclusion means changing who participates, not how loudly you announce.`,
                    coach_r2_hubclosed:     `<strong>Closing hub-level cost and growth data wastes your safest ownership lever.</strong> This is exactly the class you can share with managers to make the gap undeniable and locally owned. Gate it to the forum rather than locking it away.`,
                    coach_r2_marginopen:    `<strong>Margin-by-customer cannot be open.</strong> A competitor just poached two accounts; disclose margin-by-customer to everyone and you hand the bank, mid-covenant, a map of your weakest customers. That moves your cost of debt. Close or tightly gate it.`,
                    coach_r2_pipelineopen:  `<strong>The M&A pipeline is the most price-sensitive class you hold.</strong> Open it to 1,300 people and it reaches the market — exactly what the PE confidentiality directive was issued to prevent. This class stays closed.`,
                    coach_r2_underfund:     `<strong>Participation without facilitation is theatre.</strong> A forum or jam with no logistics, trained facilitation or calibration support produces a meeting, not ownership. Fund the engine of inclusion or the inclusion is nominal.`,
                    coach_r2_calibrated:    `<strong>That is a calibrated disclosure architecture.</strong> You opened participation where it builds ownership and kept the board-sensitive classes — margin-by-customer and the M&A pipeline — out of the open. Energy without leaking what moves the bank: exactly the balance.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid is the strongest control architecture here.</strong> A BSC strategy-map cascade gives the board its diagnostic line of sight while OKR-style bottom-up commitments carry the ownership the opening created. It links objectives to measures, targets and owners on both sides of the table.`,
                    coach_r3_bsc:           `<strong>A pure Balanced Scorecard cascade gives clean board control</strong> and a clear objectives-to-measures-to-owners line — but on its own it can feel top-down. Pair it with bottom-up commitments so the hubs own the targets, not just receive them.`,
                    coach_r3_okr:           `<strong>A pure OKR system maximises bottom-up ownership</strong> — strong on inclusion — but the PE board still needs a diagnostic line of sight on covenant-critical measures. Add the scorecard discipline so control does not slip.`,
                    coach_r3_control:       `<strong>A 40-metric, board-only diagnostic reproduces the disengagement.</strong> It will tell you precisely how the plan fails, because nobody below the board can see or shape it. Where are the interactive, shared measures that let hub directors steer rather than just be scored?`,
                    coach_r3_diagnostic:    `<strong>Diagnostic-only control scores people without involving them.</strong> Simons' point is that interactive controls — shared measures managers debate and steer by — are what build alignment. A dashboard that only scores from the top reproduces the closed process in a prettier format.`,
                    coach_r3_beliefs:       `<strong>Beliefs alone don't run a refinancing.</strong> Mission and values matter, but with no hard measures, targets or owners the board and the bank have nothing to rely on, and the 11% forecast bias goes uncorrected. You need diagnostic and interactive levers, not values posters.`,
                    coach_r3_alltransparent:`<strong>Sharing every measure widely over-discloses.</strong> Some measures — margin, covenant headroom — carry board-sensitive information; broadcasting all 7–8 raises leak and covenant risk for little extra ownership. Keep the sensitive few board-only.`,
                    coach_r3_allboard:      `<strong>Keeping almost everything board-only starves ownership.</strong> If hub directors can see only one shared measure, the dashboard scores them rather than letting them steer. Share the operational measures widely; reserve only the truly sensitive ones.`,
                    coach_r3_calibtopdown:  `<strong>Overriding the numbers top-down hits the plan but kills the ownership.</strong> Reference-class forecasting and peer review counter the 11% bias <em>with</em> the managers; an imposed override is the handed-down number in a new wrapper.`,
                    coach_r3_calibnone:     `<strong>No calibration leaves the 11% bias intact.</strong> Bottom-up numbers without shared logic drift — managers low-ball or over-promise — and the board can't trust the plan against the covenant. Build in peer review and reference-class forecasting.`,
                    coach_r4_regate:        `<strong>That treats the leak as the architecture problem it is.</strong> The leak happened because a data class was opened that should have been gated. Finding and re-gating that class fixes the cause without re-closing the participation that finally created ownership.`,
                    coach_r4_press:         `<strong>A press statement does not fix the architecture.</strong> The plan leaked because you opened a class you should have gated; a statement manages optics while the gate stays broken. Re-gate the failed class, don't just communicate around it.`,
                    coach_r4_reclose:       `<strong>Re-closing the whole process throws away the ownership you built.</strong> One leak does not justify going back behind the door — that returns you to 24%/19% and the flat year. Re-gate the single class that failed, not the entire opening.`,
                    coach_r4_withhubs:      `<strong>That is reconciliation, not imposition.</strong> Showing the hub directors the covenant and finding the 4% together preserves the ownership the opened process created while still meeting the bank's requirement. The number is closed <em>with</em> them, not over them.`,
                    coach_r4_impose:        `<strong>Imposing the 4% top-down restores the handed-down number.</strong> You close the gap on paper and destroy the buy-in you just built — straight back to a plan nobody owns. Reconcile the gap with the hub directors instead.`,
                    coach_r4_accept:        `<strong>Accepting a 4% shortfall risks the covenant.</strong> Ownership is high, but a plan 4% below the refinancing requirement narrows headroom toward the 3.25x limit and rattles the bank. Reconcile the gap with the hubs rather than letting it ride.`,
                    coach_r4_calibrated:    `<strong>That is a board-ready defence of calibrated openness.</strong> You showed the chair the classes you kept closed and the gate you re-gated, and the CEO the control levers that keep him in command — opened enough to own the plan, controlled enough to refinance. That is the whole point.`,
                    coach_r4_cavechair:     `<strong>Caving to the chair re-closes the process.</strong> Removing all leak risk by going back behind the door also removes the ownership — you trade the flat year's disengagement back in. Defend the calibration: gated, not closed.`,
                    coach_r4_caveceo:       `<strong>Caving to the CEO keeps strategy a leadership privilege.</strong> That is precisely the closed process that produced the stagnation. Calibrated openness means the CEO keeps control of the sensitive classes and the levers — not control of who may participate at all.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed the inclusion deficit; protected confidentiality.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-read the dimensions or what closure protects.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Calibrated participation; board-sensitive classes kept closed.`,
                    sum_r2_leak:            `Opened a board-sensitive class — leak & covenant risk.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Participation or disclosure under-calibrated.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Hybrid control system, shared + board-only, calibrated.`,
                    sum_r3_control:        `Control-only dashboard — reproduces disengagement.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Dashboard balance off — ownership or control thin.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Re-gated the leak, reconciled with the hubs, held the line.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Re-closed, imposed, or treated the leak as comms.`,

                    insight_calibrated:     `Calibrated open strategy: you opened the dimension Chimiq actually lacked — inclusion — while gating the data that moves the bank, built a dashboard the hubs can steer by, and reconciled the 4% with the directors rather than over them. Ownership rose without breaching the covenant. That is the art of bounded openness.`,
                    insight_radical:        `Radical-transparency backfire: leak/governance risk ran hot and board confidence collapsed. Opening sensitive classes to everyone energised the firm right up to the moment the bank, mid-refinancing, found your margin and pipeline data on a trade forum. Open strategy is calibrated, not maximal.`,
                    insight_reclosed:       `Reclosed: inclusion stayed low because you cascaded the same six-person plan or retreated behind the door at the first shock. Better communication of a plan nobody helped build does not create ownership — and you are back toward the flat 1–2% year.`,
                    insight_unbalanced:     `Opened but unbalanced: you moved on some dimensions but left a lever — inclusion, control, the disclosure matrix or the reconciliation — under-calibrated. Open strategy is the deliberate balance of all four, not a strong move on one.`,
                    insight_breach:         `You let the bottom-up plan ride 4% below the refinancing requirement: covenant headroom narrowed toward the 3.25x limit and the bank priced in the risk. Honest targets still have to be reconciled with hard external constraints.`,
                    insight_lowinclusion:   `Employee inclusion stayed low: the people closest to customers and costs still did not help make the strategy, so the ownership deficit behind the 24%/19% pulse persists.`,
                    insight_leak:           `Leak / governance risk ended elevated: a data class that should have been gated or closed stayed exposed, and under a live covenant negotiation that has a real cost of debt.`,
                    insight_reconciled:     `You closed the 4% gap with the hub directors, not over them — the rare outcome where targets are both owned and sufficient for the refinancing. Procedural justice and the covenant satisfied at once.`,

                    hint_leak:              `Leak / governance risk is critical — a board-sensitive data class is over-exposed. Re-gate margin-by-customer and the M&A pipeline before the bank prices it into your cost of debt.`,
                    hint_inclusion:         `Inclusion is dangerously low — you are reproducing the closed process. Change who participates in making strategy, not just who hears the cascade.`,
                    hint_control:           `Board and covenant confidence is low. Calibrate your openness: keep board-sensitive classes closed and give the board a diagnostic line of sight on covenant-critical measures.`,
                    hint_target:            `Target attainment is short of the refinancing plan. Build calibration against the 11% forecast bias and reconcile the gap with the hub directors.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong, calibrated position — inclusion and control both healthy. Now hold the line in the recovery test: re-gate the leak and reconcile the 4% with the hubs.`,

